  "problem": "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}3x, & x\\ne 4 \\\\ 13, & x=4,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {4}^{+}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to {4}^{-}}g(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=g(4)$\n- $g(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=4$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $g(4)=13$ exists$\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{-}}3x=12, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}3x=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude that statement I is true and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=12.\\,\\,\u2713\n$$\n\n- The value of the limit is ${lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)=12,$ while the value of the function is $g(4)=13.$ So, statement II is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 4}g(x)\\ne g(4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the third condition failed, the function has a removable discontinuity at $x=4,$ and statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true.",
